The behavior seen here seems correct although not intuitive.  My guess=20
is that your database executables where created with locale support=20
enabled and your database was inited with a US locale.  The sorting=20
logic for the english locales only sort on alpha-numeric characters. =20
All other characters are ignored for sorting purposes.

So these values:

000-0987877374-00313
___-0987877410-00316
___-0987877430-00317
100-0987877381-00314
100-0987877395-00315
200-0987877461-00318
get sorted as:

000098787737400313
098787741000316
098787743000317
100098787738100314
100098787739500315
200098787746100318
thus you see it is sorted correctly.  If you look at all of the other=20
examples you will see this explains all of the results you are seeing.

What I think you really want here is a binary sort order, not a locale=20
specific sort order.  So you should initdb your database with a locale=20
of C, or rebuild postgresql without locale support enabled.

I think there is a contrib program that you can use to see what locale=20
your database was created with.  Once your database is created you can't=20
change the locale, you will need to re-initdb.

thanks,
--Barry

